<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · date unknown · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Chakrulo</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ka">ჩაკრულო</bdi> / <bdi lang="ka-Latn">Chak'rulo</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Georgian folk song sung in three parts that was sent into space on the Voyager spacecraft in 1977.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Georgians of Kakheti</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Kakheti, Georgia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrulo">Chakrulo</a>&quot; is a Georgian polyphonic choral folk song from the region of Kakheti. It is sung in three parts and dramatises the preparations for a battle. Two highly ornamented solo voices sing over a choral foundation.</p><p>In 1977 it was one of the 27 pieces of music on the Voyager Golden Records, sent into space aboard Voyager 2 on 20 August 1977 and Voyager 1 on 5 September 1977. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognised Georgian vocal polyphony as a masterpiece of Intangible Heritage in 2001, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrulo">Chakrulo</a>&quot; was cited as a prime example.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of men readying themselves for battle. Its mood is grave and resolute, carried by two richly ornamented voices above a steady chorus.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition of them has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above tells what they are about.</p></section>
